## Why Asked
This question evaluates understanding of fundamental sorting algorithms and their practical trade-offs in real-world applications.

## Key Concepts
- Divide and conquer strategies
- Partitioning vs merging approaches
- In-place vs auxiliary space requirements
- Algorithm stability and performance characteristics

## Code Example
```
// Quicksort partition
function partition(arr, low, high) {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}

// Mergesort merge
function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      result.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding three-way partitioning shows depth beyond basic quicksort, especially for duplicates. It also tests in-place mutation and careful loop invariants.

## Key Concepts

- Three-way partitioning around a pivot
- In-place sorting with O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case time, handling duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(arr, left=0, right=arr.length-1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const pivot = arr[Math.floor((left + right) / 2)];
  let lt = left, i = left, gt = right;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, left, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, gt + 1, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you mitigate worst-case performance in practice?
- How would you adapt the algorithm to a custom comparator or stable variants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP, memoization, and bottom-up tabulation on a classic polygon triangulation problem with solution counting. It checks careful state design and combinatorial counting under optimal substructure.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on polygons: dp[i][j] = min over i<k<j of dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + w[i]*w[k]*w[j]
- Counting optimal solutions: parallel cnt[i][j] updated when a new min is found or matched
- Circular handling: fix a root vertex and solve a linearized version

## Code Example
```javascript
function minTriangulation(w){
  const n = w.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const cnt = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n-1;i++){ dp[i][i+1] = 0; cnt[i][i+1] = 1; }
  for(let len=2; len<n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len<n; i++){
      const j = i+len;
      for(let k=i+1; k<j; k++){
        const val = dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + w[i]*w[k]*w[j];
        if(val < dp[i][j]){ dp[i][j] = val; cnt[i][j] = cnt[i][k] * cnt[k][j]; }
        else if(val === dp[i][j]){ cnt[i][j] += cnt[i][k] * cnt[k][j]; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {minCost: dp[0][n-1], ways: cnt[0][n-1]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Design a structure to maintain the k-th largest element efficiently in a streaming context, using a heap and a fixed-size container.

## Key Concepts
- Min-heap for thresholding top-k
- Time: O(log k) per insert
- Edge cases: fewer than k elements, duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthLargest {
  constructor(k, nums = []) {
    this.k = k; this.heap = [];
    for (const n of nums) this.add(n);
  }
  add(val) {
    if (this.heap.length < this.k) { this.heap.push(val); this._bubbleUp(this.heap.length-1); }
    else if (val > this.heap[0]) { this.heap[0] = val; this._siftDown(0); }
    return this.heap.length === this.k ? this.heap[0] : null;
  }
  // ... helpers omitted for brevity
}
```

