## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

## Why This Is Asked

Quicksort with many duplicates can degrade; 3-way partitioning reduces swaps and comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- In-place iterative sorting (no recursion)
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid degeneracy
- Space: O(log n) for manual stack

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3WayIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [];
  stack.push([0, arr.length - 1]);
  while (stack.length) {
    const [l, r] = stack.pop();
    if (l >= r) continue;
    const m = l + Math.floor((r - l) / 2);
    // median-of-three to pick pivot
    if (arr[l] > arr[m]) [arr[l], arr[m]] = [arr[m], arr[l]];
    if (arr[l] > arr[r]) [arr[l], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[l]];
    if (arr[m] > arr[r]) [arr[m], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[m]];
    const pivot = arr[m];
    // Dutch National Flag partition
    let i = l, lt = l, gt = r;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    stack.push([l, lt - 1]);
    stack.push([gt + 1, r]);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of introsort-style hybrids, balancing average performance with worst-case guarantees in production environments. It tests pivot strategies, partitioning, depth tracking, and in-place constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth-based switch
- Median-of-three pivot: reduces chance of worst-case partitions
- 3-way partitioning: handles duplicates efficiently
- Insertion sort for tiny subarrays: amortized gains

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length)) * 2;
  function insertionSort(a, lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i < hi; i++) {
      let key = a[i], j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function heapify(a, count, i, offset) {
    // placeholder for in-place heapify within [offset, offset+count)
  }
  function partition(a, lo, hi) {
    // median-of-three and 3-way partitioning placeholder
    return Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
  }
  function sort(a, lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo <= 16) { insertionSort(a, lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth > maxDepth) { /* heapsort(a, lo, hi); */ return; }
    const pivot = partition(a, lo, hi);
    // recursively sort partitions
    sort(a, lo, pivot, depth + 1);
    sort(a, pivot, hi, depth + 1);
  }
  sort(arr, 0, arr.length, 0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to be stable, or explain why stability is costly in-place?
- Compare this approach with TimSort or other stable hybrids in real-world libraries.
- What are the practical pitfalls when implementing pivot selection and depth bounds in a multi-threaded setting?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement non-recursive mergesort on a linked list, a common real-world pattern where recursion is costly or prohibited. Tests pointer manipulation, stability of merge, and correctness of bottom-up passes, plus O(n log n) time and O(1) extra space.

## Key Concepts

- Bottom-up mergesort
- In-place linked-list merge
- Stability
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function mergeTwo(l1, l2) {
  const dummy = { val: 0, next: null };
  let tail = dummy;
  while (l1 && l2) {
    if (l1.val <= l2.val) {
      tail.next = l1;
      l1 = l1.next;
    } else {
      tail.next = l2;
      l2 = l2.next;
    }
    tail = tail.next;
  }
  tail.next = l1 || l2;
  return dummy.next;
}

function sortList(head) {
  if (!head || !head.next) return head;
  // length
  let len = 0, p = head;
  while (p) { len++; p = p.next; }
  const dummy = { next: head };
  for (let w = 1; w < len; w <<= 1) {
    let prev = dummy;
    let curr = dummy.next;
    while (curr) {
      // split left
      let left = curr;
      let i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const right = curr.next;
      curr.next = null;
      // split right
      curr = right;
      i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const next = curr ? curr.next : null;
      if (curr) curr.next = null;
      const merged = mergeTwo(left, right);
      prev.next = merged;
      while (prev.next) prev = prev.next;
      curr = next;
    }
  }
  return dummy.next;
}
```

